    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the start of this episode, the hospital doctor tells Katya, Elena, and Eleonora that Chef Barinov needs an urgent operation in Germany costing two hundred and fifty thousand euros. With her own assets tied up in a loan, Eleonora promises to ask a wealthy friend for help.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Его состояние стабильно тяжёлое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Слава богу, такое сейчас лечится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Нужна срочная операция в Германии.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "По подсчётам это стоит дорого.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня нет таких денег.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Деньги не проблема, да, мама?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Вообще-то это большая проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но ведь он мой отец.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Я отель в Питере построила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Я большой кредит под залог взяла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я могу свою квартиру продать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Не надо ничего сейчас продавать.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Его состояние стабильно тяжёлое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'His condition is stable but serious.' the doctor anchors the line on 'his', while 'heavy' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Слава богу, такое сейчас лечится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank goodness, this is treatable now.' The meaning hinges on 'glory' working together with 'is treatable' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Нужна срочная операция в Германии.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'An urgent operation in Germany is needed.' The meaning hinges on 'needed' working together with 'Germany' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "По подсчётам это стоит дорого.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'By estimates this costs a lot.' In Russian, the idea of 'according to' flows naturally into 'expensive' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня нет таких денег.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not have that kind of money.' In Russian, the idea of 'at' flows naturally into 'money' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Деньги не проблема, да, мама?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Money is not a problem, right, Mom?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'money' and finishing on 'mom'. Eleonora answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Вообще-то это большая проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Actually, it is a big problem.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'actually' before landing on 'problem'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но ведь он мой отец.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But after all, he is my father.' Katya brings out the contrast between 'but' and 'after all' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Я отель в Питере построила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I built a hotel in Saint Petersburg.' Notice how Russian expresses 'I' and 'built' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Я большой кредит под залог взяла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I took out a big loan against collateral.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'picked up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я могу свою квартиру продать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I can sell my apartment.' The meaning hinges on 'I' working together with 'to sell' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Не надо ничего сейчас продавать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There is no need to sell anything right now.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'to sell' to negate the statement. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
